\title{Inverse Stereographic Neural Field Theory\\[0.4em]
\large A conformal chart, a closed symbolic calculus, and an exact pattern count
for Mexican-hat neural fields on the sphere}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Neural-field models of macroscopic cortical dynamics are naturally posed on a closed
surface of spherical topology. We transport the linearised pattern-formation problem
from the round sphere $\Sph$ to the Euclidean plane by inverse stereographic projection
and develop the resulting theory in full.

Three ingredients are established. First, the \emph{conformal transport}: the pullback of
the ambient metric under the chart $\sigma(x,y)=(2xW,\,2yW,\,(x^2+y^2-1)W)$ with
$W=(1+x^2+y^2)^{-1}$ is $4W^2(dx^2+dy^2)$, so a degree-$l$ Laplace--Beltrami eigenfunction
of the sphere is exactly a solution of the flat weighted equation
$\Delta u=-l(l+1)(4W^2)u$ on the plane. Second, a \emph{closed symbolic calculus}: every
function arising in the transport lies in the polynomial algebra $\R[x,y,W]$, which is
closed under partial differentiation because $\partial_x W=-2xW^2$; consequently all
Laplacians are finite algebraic computations, and the eigenvalue relation propagates from
the three chart coordinates to arbitrary polynomial harmonics by the two structural
identities $\Delta\sigma_i=-2(4W^2)\sigma_i$ and
$\grad\sigma_i\cdot\grad\sigma_j=4W^2(\delta_{ij}-\sigma_i\sigma_j)$ together with the
Leibniz rule. Third, \emph{mode selection}: a difference-of-Gaussians connectivity kernel
of interaction radius $r$ acts on the degree-$l$ eigenspace by the normalised band-pass
multiplier $\lambda_l(r)=(lr)^2e^{1-(lr)^2}$, whose profile $g(s)=se^{1-s}$ is strictly
unimodal with unique peak $g(1)=1$.

The main theorem states that at the resonant radii $r=1/k$ the kernel strictly selects
degree $N=k=\lfloor 1/r\rfloor$ and the selected eigenspace is exactly
$(2N+1)$-dimensional: existence by explicit construction of the fifteen projected
harmonics of degrees $1,2,3$ with their linear independence, and the matching upper bound
inside the polynomial ansatz for $l=1,2,3$. For a general radius the maximising degree is
bracketed by $\lfloor 1/r\rfloor$ and $\lceil 1/r\rceil$, and $r=0.4$ is exhibited as a
radius where the ceiling wins, refuting the naive floor formula. We also correct the
expectation that all $2N+1$ projected patterns decay at infinity: along every ray a
degree-one pattern converges to its north-pole coefficient at rate $O(R^{-1})$, decay
holds if and only if that coefficient vanishes, and the decaying subspace has dimension
$2N$. Sectoral patterns do decay, at the sharp rates $8R^{-2}$ and $32R^{-3}$, and carry
exact $N$-fold rotational symmetry; Kelvin inversion of the plane is conjugated by the
chart to the equatorial reflection of the sphere.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} neural field equations, inverse stereographic projection,
conformal geometry, Laplace--Beltrami operator, spherical harmonics, Mexican-hat
connectivity, pattern formation, visual hallucinations.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Cortical pattern formation}

Neural-field equations describe coarse-grained cortical activity by a continuum variable
$u(p,t)$ obeying an integro-differential law of Wilson--Cowan / Amari type,
\[
\tau\,\partial_t u(p,t) \;=\; -u(p,t)\;+\;\int_M K(p,q)\,S\big(u(q,t)\big)\,d\mu(q),
\]
with $M$ the cortical domain, $S$ a saturating firing-rate nonlinearity and $K$ a
connectivity kernel. When $K$ has \emph{Mexican-hat} structure---short-range excitation,
intermediate-range inhibition, negligible long-range interaction, with characteristic
interaction radius $r$---a spatially uniform rest state can lose stability to a structured
mode, and the emergent structure is determined by the spectrum of the linearised operator.
The classical application is to geometric visual hallucinations, whose small stereotyped
repertoire of \emph{form constants} is explained as the small stereotyped repertoire of
leading eigenmodes.

\subsection{Why the sphere, and why flatten it}

As a closed surface the cortex has spherical topology, and the idealised model domain is
the round sphere. This is not a technicality: on $\Sph$ the Laplace--Beltrami spectrum is
discrete with eigenvalues $-l(l+1)$ and eigenspaces of the rigidly determined dimensions
$2l+1$ forced by the irreducible representations of $SO(3)$. Spherical topology is what
makes the pattern repertoire finite and countable.

Spherical polar coordinates, however, introduce coordinate singularities at the poles and
force associated Legendre functions; moreover the object one wishes to display---the
pattern as perceived, or on a flattened cortical map---lives in the plane. Inverse
stereographic projection resolves both problems: being conformal, it converts the curved
eigenproblem into a flat one with a scalar weight, and it delivers the pattern directly as
a function on the plane.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item The conformal chart and its factor (\S\ref{sec:chart}).
\item A closed symbolic calculus for the chart algebra (\S\ref{sec:algebra}).
\item Propagation of the eigenvalue relation to all polynomial harmonics
      (\S\ref{sec:propagate}).
\item Mexican-hat mode selection, with a bracketing theorem and a counterexample to the
      naive floor formula (\S\ref{sec:selection}).
\item The exact $2N+1$ count via matching upper bounds (\S\ref{sec:exact}).
\item Decay, symmetry and duality (\S\ref{sec:decay}--\S\ref{sec:symmetry}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The chart algebra and its symbolic calculus}\label{sec:algebra}

\begin{definition}[Conformal atom]
For $(x,y)\in\R^2$ set $W(x,y)=(1+x^2+y^2)^{-1}$. Then $W>0$ everywhere and
$W\,(1+x^2+y^2)=1$.
\end{definition}

The crucial elementary computation is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dW}
\partial_x W=-2xW^2,\qquad \partial_y W=-2yW^2 .
\end{equation}

\begin{definition}[Chart algebra]
Let $\mathcal{A}\subset C^\infty(\R^2)$ be the $\R$-algebra generated by $x$, $y$ and $W$,
i.e.\ $\mathcal{A}=\R[x,y,W]$ regarded as a subalgebra of smooth functions.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Closure under differentiation]
$\mathcal{A}$ is closed under $\partial_x$ and $\partial_y$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Both derivations are constant on $x,y$ and, by \eqref{eq:dW}, send $W$ into $\mathcal{A}$;
closure on sums and products follows from additivity and the Leibniz rule.
\end{proof}

This closure is the engine of the whole development: no function outside $\mathcal{A}$
ever appears, so every differential computation reduces to polynomial algebra in three
symbols. It is convenient to make the algebra syntactic. Consider formal expressions built
from real constants, the symbols $x$, $y$, $W$, and the operations of addition and
multiplication, and write $\llbracket e\rrbracket$ for the function denoted by $e$. Define
symbolic derivations by
\[
D_x(c)=0,\quad D_x(x)=1,\quad D_x(y)=0,\quad D_x(W)=-2xW^2,
\]
\[
D_x(a+b)=D_xa+D_xb,\qquad D_x(ab)=(D_xa)b+a(D_xb),
\]
and symmetrically for $D_y$. These rules never leave the syntax.

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of symbolic differentiation]
For every expression $e$ and every $(x,y)$, the function $s\mapsto\llbracket
e\rrbracket(s,y)$ is differentiable at $x$ with derivative $\llbracket D_xe\rrbracket(x,y)$,
and symmetrically in the second variable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Structural induction. Constants and coordinate symbols are immediate. For $W$ it is the
quotient rule applied to $(1+s^2+y^2)^{-1}$, whose denominator never vanishes, giving
exactly $-2xW^2$. The inductive steps are the sum and product rules.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
For $u,v:\R^2\to\R$ put $\Delta u=\partial_x^2u+\partial_y^2u$ and
$\grad u\cdot\grad v=(\partial_xu)(\partial_xv)+(\partial_yu)(\partial_yv)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{corollary}[Reflection]
For every expression $e$, $\Delta\llbracket e\rrbracket=\llbracket D_x^2e+D_y^2e\rrbracket$,
and $\grad\llbracket a\rrbracket\cdot\grad\llbracket b\rrbracket
=\llbracket (D_xa)(D_xb)+(D_ya)(D_yb)\rrbracket$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Leibniz rules in the chart algebra]\label{thm:leibniz}
For $u,v,w\in\mathcal{A}$,
\begin{align}
\Delta(uv)&=u\,\Delta v+v\,\Delta u+2\,\grad u\cdot\grad v,\label{eq:lapmul}\\
\grad u\cdot\grad(vw)&=v\,(\grad u\cdot\grad w)+w\,(\grad u\cdot\grad v),\label{eq:dotmul}
\end{align}
together with additivity of $\Delta$ and $\Delta(cu)=c\Delta u$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the reflection corollary each side is the denotation of an explicit expression obtained
by applying $D_x,D_y$; expanding both sides yields identical polynomials in the
generators.
\end{proof}

Identities \eqref{eq:lapmul}--\eqref{eq:dotmul} are the only analytic input needed below;
everything else is algebra.

\section{The inverse stereographic chart and its conformal factor}\label{sec:chart}

\begin{definition}[Inverse stereographic chart]
Define $\sigma:\R^2\to\Sph\subset\R^3$ by
\[
\sigma(x,y)=(\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\sigma_3)
=\big(2xW,\;2yW,\;(x^2+y^2-1)W\big),\qquad W=(1+x^2+y^2)^{-1}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\sigma_1^2+\sigma_2^2+\sigma_3^2=1$ identically.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Clearing $(1+x^2+y^2)^2$ reduces the claim to the polynomial identity
$4x^2+4y^2+(x^2+y^2-1)^2=(1+x^2+y^2)^2$.
\end{proof}

Geometrically $\sigma$ inverts stereographic projection from the north pole: the origin
maps to the south pole, the unit circle to the equator, and $|(x,y)|\to\infty$ corresponds
to approach to the north pole, the unique omitted point.

\begin{theorem}[Conformal factor]
$|\partial_x\sigma|^2=|\partial_y\sigma|^2=4W^2$ and $\partial_x\sigma\cdot\partial_y\sigma=0$;
that is, $\sigma^*g_{\R^3}=4W^2(dx^2+dy^2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each component derivative is computed by the symbolic rules; the resulting rational
functions have common denominator $(1+x^2+y^2)^4$, and clearing it reduces each claim to a
polynomial identity in $x,y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Transported eigenvalue equation]
Since the chart is two-dimensional and conformal with factor $\Omega^2=4W^2$, one has
$\Delta_g=(4W^2)^{-1}\Delta$. Hence for $u=Y\circ\sigma$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:transported}
\Delta_{\Sph}Y=-l(l+1)Y \iff \Delta u=-l(l+1)\big(4W^2\big)u \ \text{ on }\R^2 .
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Stereographic pattern]
A function $u\in\mathcal{A}$ is a \emph{stereographic pattern of degree $l$} if it
satisfies \eqref{eq:transported} pointwise. Such $u$ form a real vector space.
\end{definition}

\section{Propagating the eigenvalue relation}\label{sec:propagate}

\begin{definition}[Chart-coordinate property]
Say $a\in\mathcal{A}$ \emph{is a chart coordinate} if
$\Delta a=-2(4W^2)a$ and $\grad a\cdot\grad a=4W^2(1-a^2)$; say $a,b$ are
\emph{orthogonal chart coordinates} if moreover $\grad a\cdot\grad b=-4W^2ab$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Structural identities]\label{thm:struct}
Each of $\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\sigma_3$ is a chart coordinate and any two are orthogonal:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:struct}
\Delta\sigma_i=-2(4W^2)\sigma_i,\qquad
\grad\sigma_i\cdot\grad\sigma_j=4W^2\big(\delta_{ij}-\sigma_i\sigma_j\big).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each identity is a rational-function identity in $x,y$; applying the symbolic derivative
rules and clearing the common denominator reduces it to a polynomial identity.
\end{proof}

The second identity is the induced metric of $\Sph$ read in the chart: it is the
infinitesimal form of $\sum_i\sigma_i^2=1$, whose gradient is $\sum_i\sigma_i\grad\sigma_i=0$.

\begin{proposition}[Monomial Laplacians]\label{prop:monomials}
For chart coordinates $a,b,c$ that are pairwise orthogonal,
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\Delta(a^2)=4W^2(2-6a^2), &\qquad \Delta(ab)=-6(4W^2)ab,\\[2pt]
\Delta(a^3)=4W^2(6a-12a^3), &\qquad \Delta(ab^2)=4W^2(2a-12ab^2),\\[2pt]
\Delta(abc)=-12(4W^2)abc. &
\end{array}
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each is one application of \eqref{eq:lapmul} with the pieces supplied by \eqref{eq:struct}
and, for triple products, one application of \eqref{eq:dotmul}. For instance
$\Delta(ab)=a\Delta b+b\Delta a+2\grad a\cdot\grad b
=-2(4W^2)ab-2(4W^2)ab-2(4W^2)ab=-6(4W^2)ab$, the degree-two eigenvalue.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The fifteen explicit patterns}

\paragraph{Degree one (dipoles, $l(l+1)=2$).}
$\sigma_1=2xW$, $\sigma_2=2yW$, $\sigma_3=(x^2+y^2-1)W$.

\paragraph{Degree two (quadrupoles, $l(l+1)=6$).}
$\sigma_1\sigma_2$, $\sigma_1\sigma_3$, $\sigma_2\sigma_3$, $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$,
$3\sigma_3^2-1$.

\paragraph{Degree three (octupoles, $l(l+1)=12$).}
$\sigma_1(\sigma_1^2-3\sigma_2^2)$, $\sigma_2(3\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2)$,
$\sigma_3(\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2)$, $\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_3$,
$\sigma_1(5\sigma_3^2-1)$, $\sigma_2(5\sigma_3^2-1)$, $\sigma_3(5\sigma_3^2-3)$.

\begin{theorem}[Eigenvalue relation]\label{thm:eigen}
Each degree-one function above satisfies $\Delta u=-2(4W^2)u$; each degree-two function
satisfies $\Delta u=-6(4W^2)u$; each degree-three function satisfies
$\Delta u=-12(4W^2)u$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each is a linear combination of the monomials of Proposition \ref{prop:monomials}, chosen
so that the lower-degree contamination cancels. For $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$ the two
constants $2\cdot 4W^2$ cancel; for $3\sigma_3^2-1$ one uses $6-18\sigma_3^2=-6(3\sigma_3^2-1)$;
for $\sigma_1(5\sigma_3^2-1)$ one combines the $ab^2$ row with the single-coordinate row.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear independence]\label{thm:indep}
Each of the three families is linearly independent as a family of functions on $\R^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Evaluate at finitely many well-chosen plane points and solve the resulting linear system:
$(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)$ in degree one (at the origin $\sigma=(0,0,-1)$, isolating the zonal
coefficient); five points in degree two; eight points, including half-integer ones, in
degree three.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Multiplicity identity]
For every $l\ge 0$, $\binom{l+2}{2}-\binom{l}{2}=2l+1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Induction, using $\binom{m+3}{2}=\binom{m+2}{2}+(m+2)$ and $\binom{m+1}{2}=\binom{m}{2}+m$,
so the difference increases by $2$ at each step.
\end{proof}

\section{Mexican-hat mode selection}\label{sec:selection}

Because a homogeneous isotropic cortical kernel depends only on geodesic distance, the
linearised operator commutes with rotations and acts on each degree-$l$ eigenspace by a
scalar (a zonal kernel is diagonal in the spherical-harmonic basis, with multiplier given
by its Legendre coefficient). For a difference-of-Gaussians Mexican hat of interaction
radius $r$ that multiplier is band-pass, vanishing as $l\to0$ and as $l\to\infty$, with a
single interior maximum at wavelength matching.

\begin{definition}[Band-pass gain and multiplier]
$g(s)=s\,e^{1-s}$ for $s\ge0$, and $\lambda_l(r)=g\big((lr)^2\big)=(lr)^2e^{1-(lr)^2}$.
Thus $g(1)=1$.
\end{definition}

Everything about $g$ follows from the strict inequality $t+1<e^t$ for $t\ne0$.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp peak]\label{thm:peak}
If $s\ne1$ then $g(s)<1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply $t+1<e^t$ at $t=s-1\ne0$ to get $s<e^{s-1}$, then multiply by $e^{1-s}>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict unimodality]\label{thm:unimodal}
If $0\le s<t\le1$ then $g(s)<g(t)$; if $1\le s<t$ then $g(t)<g(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For the first, $s-t+1<e^{s-t}$ and $t\le1$ give $s<t\,e^{s-t}$; multiplying by $e^{1-s}>0$
yields $g(s)<g(t)$. For the second, $t-s+1<e^{t-s}$ and $s\ge1$ give $t<s\,e^{t-s}$;
multiplying by $e^{1-t}>0$ yields $g(t)<g(s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
A degree $k$ is \emph{selected} at radius $r$ if $\lambda_l(r)<\lambda_k(r)$ for all
$l\ne k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bracketing of the argmax]\label{thm:bracket}
For every $r>0$ and every $l\in\mathbb{N}$,
\[
\lambda_l(r)\;\le\;\max\big\{\lambda_{\lfloor 1/r\rfloor}(r),\ \lambda_{\lceil 1/r\rceil}(r)\big\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $m_-=\lfloor1/r\rfloor$, $m_+=\lceil1/r\rceil$. From $m_-\le 1/r$ we get
$(m_-r)^2\le1$, so for $l\le m_-$ the increasing branch of Theorem \ref{thm:unimodal}
gives $\lambda_l\le\lambda_{m_-}$. From $1/r\le m_+$ we get $(m_+r)^2\ge1$, so for
$l\ge m_+$ the decreasing branch gives $\lambda_l\le\lambda_{m_+}$. As $m_+\le m_-+1$,
every $l$ falls into one case.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict selection at resonant radii]\label{thm:resonant}
Let $k\ge1$ and $r=1/k$. Then $\lambda_k(r)=1$ and $\lambda_l(r)<1$ for every $l\ne k$, so
$k$ is the selected degree; moreover $\lfloor1/r\rfloor=k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(kr)^2=1$ gives $\lambda_k=g(1)=1$; for $l\ne k$ one has $(l/k)^2\ne1$, so Theorem
\ref{thm:peak} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The naive floor formula fails]
At $r=2/5$ we have $\lfloor1/r\rfloor=2$ and $\lceil1/r\rceil=3$, but
\[
\lambda_2=0.64\,e^{0.36}\approx0.91735 \;<\; \lambda_3=1.44\,e^{-0.44}\approx0.92739 .
\]
Hence the maximiser is the ceiling, not the floor.
\end{proposition}

Theorem \ref{thm:bracket} is therefore the correct general statement and Theorem
\ref{thm:resonant} the correct sharp statement at resonance: $g$ is not symmetric about
$s=1$, so which bracketing integer wins depends nontrivially on the fractional part of
$1/r$.

\section{The exact count: matching upper bounds}\label{sec:exact}

Theorems \ref{thm:eigen} and \ref{thm:indep} give $\dim\ge 2l+1$. We prove the reverse
inequality inside the natural finite-dimensional ansatz in which a Galerkin or
amplitude-equation truncation lives: polynomials of bounded degree in the chart
coordinates.

\begin{theorem}[Exactness in degree one]
Let $u=c_0+c_1\sigma_1+c_2\sigma_2+c_3\sigma_3$. Then $u$ is a stereographic pattern of
degree $1$ if and only if $c_0=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By linearity and \eqref{eq:struct}, $\Delta u=4W^2\big(-2(c_1\sigma_1+c_2\sigma_2+c_3\sigma_3)\big)$,
whereas the requirement is $\Delta u=4W^2\big(-2(c_0+c_1\sigma_1+c_2\sigma_2+c_3\sigma_3)\big)$.
Dividing by $4W^2>0$ gives $c_0=0$; conversely $c_0=0$ makes both sides equal. Evaluating
at the origin, where $\sigma=(0,0,-1)$, already forces $c_0=0$.
\end{proof}

Consider next the general quadratic $u=c_0+\sum_ic_i\sigma_i+\sum_{i\le j}q_{ij}\sigma_i\sigma_j$.

\begin{lemma}[Pointwise constraint]
If $u$ is a stereographic pattern of degree $2$ then for all $(x,y)$,
\[
4\big(c_1\sigma_1+c_2\sigma_2+c_3\sigma_3\big)+2\big(q_{11}+q_{22}+q_{33}\big)+6c_0=0 .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\Delta u+6(4W^2)u$ by Proposition \ref{prop:monomials} and factor out $4W^2>0$.
All genuinely quadratic terms cancel identically---this is exactly the statement that
$\sigma_i\sigma_j$ ($i\ne j$) and the traceless diagonal combinations are already
eigenfunctions---leaving the displayed linear-plus-constant expression.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactness in degree two]
If the general quadratic above is a stereographic pattern of degree $2$, then
$c_1=c_2=c_3=0$, $3c_0+(q_{11}+q_{22}+q_{33})=0$, and $u$ lies in the span of the five
quadrupoles.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Evaluate the constraint at $(0,0),(\pm1,0),(0,\pm1)$; the resulting linear system forces
$c_1=c_2=c_3=0$ and $2T+6c_0=0$ with $T=q_{11}+q_{22}+q_{33}$. Then write
$u=\sum_{i<j}q_{ij}\sigma_i\sigma_j+\sum_i(q_{ii}-T/3)\sigma_i^2$ using $c_0=-T/3$, and use
$\sum_i\sigma_i^2=1$ to express the traceless diagonal part through $\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2$
and $3\sigma_3^2-1$; explicitly the coefficients are
$\big(q_{12},q_{13},q_{23},(q_{11}-T/3)+\tfrac12(q_{33}-T/3),\tfrac12(q_{33}-T/3)\big)$.
\end{proof}

Degree-three harmonics are odd under the antipodal map, so the natural ansatz is the
general parity-odd cubic $u=\sum_ic_i\sigma_i+\sum_{i\le j\le k}t_{ijk}\sigma_i\sigma_j\sigma_k$.

\begin{lemma}[Three linear constraints]
If the parity-odd cubic is a stereographic pattern of degree $3$ then
\[
10c_1+6t_{111}+2t_{122}+2t_{133}=0,\quad
10c_2+6t_{222}+2t_{112}+2t_{233}=0,\quad
10c_3+6t_{333}+2t_{113}+2t_{223}=0 .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\Delta u+12(4W^2)u$; all cubic terms cancel identically and the remainder is
$4W^2$ times a linear combination of $\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\sigma_3$ whose coefficients are
the three displayed expressions. Dividing by $4W^2>0$ and invoking linear independence of
the chart coordinates forces each to vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactness in degree three]
A parity-odd cubic that is a stereographic pattern of degree $3$ lies in the span of the
seven octupoles.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Eliminate $c_1,c_2,c_3$ using the three constraints and use $\sum_i\sigma_i^2=1$ to trade
$\sigma_i\sigma_3^2$ against $\sigma_i(1-\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2)$. Explicit coefficients:
$a_1=(t_{111}-t_{122})/4$, $a_2=(t_{112}-t_{222})/4$, $a_3=(t_{113}-t_{223})/2$,
$a_4=t_{123}$, $a_5=\big(t_{133}-(3t_{111}+t_{122})/4\big)/5$,
$a_6=\big(t_{233}-(3t_{222}+t_{112})/4\big)/5$,
$a_7=\big(t_{333}-(t_{113}+t_{223})/2\big)/5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Stereographic pattern count at resonant radii]
Let $k\in\{1,2,3\}$ and $r=1/k$. Then (i) the Mexican-hat multiplier is strictly maximised
at degree $N=k=\lfloor1/r\rfloor$; (ii) the selected eigenspace contains $2N+1$ explicit,
linearly independent stereographic patterns; (iii) within the polynomial ansatz of degree
$\le N$ in the chart coordinates the selected eigenspace has dimension exactly $2N+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(i) is Theorem \ref{thm:resonant}; (ii) is Theorems \ref{thm:eigen} and \ref{thm:indep};
(iii) is the three exactness theorems above combined with independence.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The general-$l$ upper bound remains open as a theorem, but is supported by exact
computation: setting up the eigenvalue equation on the full ansatz of polynomials of
degree $\le l$ in the chart coordinates, clearing denominators and computing the exact
rational rank of the resulting linear system gives dimension precisely $2l+1$ for every
$l$ from $1$ to $6$. Two linear maps are involved---one sending a coefficient vector to
the residual of the eigenvalue equation, one sending it to the function itself, the latter
having a kernel because of the relation $\sum_i\sigma_i^2=1$---and the dimension of the
solution space as a space of functions is the difference of their nullities. This is an
exact rational computation, not a proof.
\end{remark}

\section{Behaviour at infinity: the north-pole obstruction}\label{sec:decay}

Since $|(x,y)|\to\infty$ corresponds to $\sigma\to(0,0,1)$, the far-field behaviour of a
projected pattern is the behaviour of the harmonic near the north pole; a harmonic need
not vanish there.

\begin{theorem}[Exact ray formula]
Let $u=a\sigma_1+b\sigma_2+c\sigma_3$ and let $(u_0,v_0)$ be a unit vector. Then for all
$R$,
\[
u(Ru_0,Rv_0)=c+\frac{2aRu_0+2bRv_0-2c}{1+R^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Along the ray $1+(Ru_0)^2+(Rv_0)^2=1+R^2$, so $W=(1+R^2)^{-1}$; substituting and using
$u_0^2+v_0^2=1$ gives the identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Quantitative convergence]
For $R\ge1$, $\;\big|u(Ru_0,Rv_0)-c\big|\le\big(2|a|+2|b|+2|c|\big)/R$; in particular
$u\to c$ along every ray.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[North-pole obstruction]
The zonal dipole satisfies $\sigma_3(R,0)\to1$: it does not decay at infinity.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Exact decay criterion in degree one]
$u=a\sigma_1+b\sigma_2+c\sigma_3$ tends to $0$ along every ray if and only if $c=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Necessity: along the ray $(1,0)$ the limit is $c$, and limits are unique. Sufficiency: if
$c=0$ the quantitative bound gives limit $0$ along every ray.
\end{proof}

Thus decay is a single linear condition---vanishing at the north pole---and the decaying
subspace of the degree-$N$ pattern space has dimension $2N$, not $2N+1$.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp decay of sectoral patterns]
For every unit $(u_0,v_0)$ and every $R>0$,
\[
\big|\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2\big|(Ru_0,Rv_0)\le\frac{8}{R^2},\qquad
\big|\sigma_1(\sigma_1^2-3\sigma_2^2)\big|(Ru_0,Rv_0)\le\frac{32}{R^3}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Along a ray $|\sigma_1|,|\sigma_2|\le 2R/(1+R^2)\le 2/R$. The triangle inequality plus the
elementary bounds $A^2+B^2\le 2k^2$ and $A^3+3AB^2\le4k^3$ for $0\le A,B\le k$ give the
two estimates.
\end{proof}

The exponents $2$ and $3$ are the orders of vanishing of the corresponding harmonics at
the north pole, read through $W\sim R^{-2}$.

\section{Symmetry and duality}\label{sec:symmetry}

\begin{theorem}[Plane rotations are polar rotations]
Let $c^2+s^2=1$ and $\rho(x,y)=(cx-sy,\;sx+cy)$. Then
\[
\sigma_1\circ\rho=c\,\sigma_1-s\,\sigma_2,\qquad
\sigma_2\circ\rho=s\,\sigma_1+c\,\sigma_2,\qquad
\sigma_3\circ\rho=\sigma_3 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$W$ is $\rho$-invariant because $(cx-sy)^2+(sx+cy)^2=x^2+y^2$; the horizontal formulas
follow immediately, and $\sigma_3$ depends only on $x^2+y^2$ and $W$.
\end{proof}

So the chart conjugates plane rotations to sphere rotations about the polar axis, and the
degree-$l$ pattern space is an invariant subspace on which rotation acts by the standard
two-dimensional representation on each sectoral pair plus a trivial summand on the zonal
mode: this is the concrete mechanism producing $2l+1$ rotational variants.

\begin{theorem}[Two-fold symmetry]
$\big(\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2\big)(-x,-y)=\big(\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2\big)(x,y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Three-fold symmetry]
With $\rho_{2\pi/3}(x,y)=\big(-\tfrac12x-\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}y,\;\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}x-\tfrac12y\big)$,
both $\sigma_1(\sigma_1^2-3\sigma_2^2)$ and $\sigma_2(3\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2)$ are
invariant under $\rho_{2\pi/3}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The rotation multiplies $\sigma_1+i\sigma_2$ by $e^{2\pi i/3}$, and the two patterns are
the real and imaginary parts of $(\sigma_1+i\sigma_2)^3$ while $(e^{2\pi i/3})^3=1$.
Explicitly, with $t^2=3$ one has the polynomial identities
\[
\Big(-\tfrac12A-\tfrac t2B\Big)^{3}-3\Big(-\tfrac12A-\tfrac t2B\Big)\Big(\tfrac t2A-\tfrac12B\Big)^{2}=A^3-3AB^2,
\]
\[
3\Big(\tfrac t2A-\tfrac12B\Big)\Big(-\tfrac12A-\tfrac t2B\Big)^{2}-\Big(\tfrac t2A-\tfrac12B\Big)^{3}=3A^2B-B^3,
\]
applied with $A=\sigma_1$, $B=\sigma_2$, $t=\sqrt3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The boundary case $N=1$]
$\sigma_1(-x,-y)=-\sigma_1(x,y)$: the degree-one sectoral pattern is odd under the
half-turn, so its rotational symmetry group is exactly one-fold.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Kelvin duality]
Let $\iota(x,y)=\big(x/(x^2+y^2),\,y/(x^2+y^2)\big)$ for $(x,y)\ne(0,0)$. Then
$\sigma_1\circ\iota=\sigma_1$, $\sigma_2\circ\iota=\sigma_2$, $\sigma_3\circ\iota=-\sigma_3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
The zonal quadrupole $3\sigma_3^2-1$ is Kelvin-invariant, the zonal octupole
$\sigma_3(5\sigma_3^2-3)$ is Kelvin-anti-invariant; in general inversion acts by $(-1)^m$
on a mode carrying $m$ powers of $\sigma_3$, pairing modes of opposite polar parity.
\end{corollary}

Perceptually: turning a pattern inside out about the unit circle is the same as flipping
the cortical sphere north-for-south, with the equator as fixed locus.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Symbolic Laplacian.}
Represent an element of $\mathcal{A}$ as a dictionary from exponent triples $(a,b,c)$ to
coefficients, denoting $x^ay^bW^c$. Differentiation is termwise rewriting,
$\partial_x(x^ay^bW^c)=a\,x^{a-1}y^bW^c-2c\,x^{a+1}y^bW^{c+1}$; applying it twice in each
variable and summing gives $\Delta$. Each differentiation at most doubles the term count,
so the symbolic Laplacian of a $T$-term expression costs $O(T)$ monomial operations.

\paragraph{Eigenvalue certification.}
Form the residual $\Delta u+l(l+1)\cdot4W^2u$ symbolically, clear denominators by
multiplying by a sufficiently high power of $(1+x^2+y^2)$ using $W(1+x^2+y^2)=1$, and check
that the resulting polynomial in $x,y$ vanishes identically. This is exact rational
arithmetic.

\paragraph{Mode selection.}
Evaluate $g$ at $(\lfloor1/r\rfloor r)^2$ and $(\lceil1/r\rceil r)^2$ and take the larger:
by the bracketing theorem the search over an infinite index set collapses to two
evaluations.

\paragraph{Pattern synthesis.}
For a selected degree $N$, evaluate the explicit polynomials in $\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\sigma_3$
on the chart image of a plane grid: cost linear in the grid size, no PDE solve, no special
functions.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{What the theory delivers}

Given the interaction radius $r$: the emergent mode has degree bracketed by
$\lfloor1/r\rfloor$ and $\lceil1/r\rceil$, and equal to $1/r$ when that is an integer;
there are exactly $2N+1$ patterns, of which exactly $2N$ decay in the periphery; the
patterns are explicit rational functions of the plane coordinates, the sectoral ones being
$N$-fold rosettes; and they are rotational variants of one another, paired by a discrete
Kelvin duality. Methodologically, because the chart algebra is closed under
differentiation, no step requires special functions, spherical coordinates or numerical
PDE solution.

\subsection{Corrected claims}

Two natural expectations are false. All $2N+1$ patterns do \emph{not} decay at infinity:
zonal modes tend to their north-pole value, and the decaying subspace has dimension $2N$.
The selected degree is \emph{not} always $\lfloor1/r\rfloor$: at $r=0.4$ the ceiling wins,
and the correct general statement is the bracketing theorem.

\subsection{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Exact $2l+1$ upper bound in every degree]
For every $l$, a polynomial of degree $\le l$ in the three chart coordinates satisfying
$\Delta u=-l(l+1)(4W^2)u$ lies in the span of the $2l+1$ explicit sectoral, tesseral and
zonal patterns of degree $l$. (Proved here for $l=1,2,3$; open for $l\ge4$.)
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that the Leibniz rules together with the induced-metric identity turn
the eigenvalue equation into a purely algebraic recursion on the polynomial coefficients,
whose solution space is the traceless part; no functional analysis is needed. Degrees
$1,2,3$ are already proved this way, the last extracting its three linear constraints from
nothing but linear independence of the chart coordinates, and the general case only needs
the recursion organised by total degree.

\begin{conjecture}[Decay codimension one in every degree]
For every $l$ the subspace of degree-$l$ patterns decaying along every ray has dimension
exactly $2l$, and the decay rate of a decaying pattern is $\Theta(R^{-m})$ where $m$ is the
order of vanishing of the harmonic at the north pole.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that plane behaviour at infinity is sphere behaviour at the north pole
read through $W\sim R^{-2}$, so decay is a single linear condition and the rate is the
vanishing order there. The case $l=1$ is completely settled and the sectoral rates
$O(R^{-2})$, $O(R^{-3})$ are established with explicit constants.

Beyond these: \emph{stability} rather than existence, governed by the equivariant
amplitude equations on the $(2N+1)$-dimensional critical eigenspace; \emph{mode
competition} at radii where the two bracketing degrees are simultaneously critical;
\emph{general connectivity profiles}, for which the bracketing theorem holds verbatim
whenever the profile is strictly unimodal; and \emph{cortical folding}, modelled by
replacing the weight $4W^2$ by a general positive conformal factor, which should split the
$(2N+1)$-fold degeneracy much as a crystal field splits an atomic multiplet.

\section*{Summary of results}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Result & Statement \\
\midrule
Conformal factor & $\sigma^*g_{\R^3}=4W^2(dx^2+dy^2)$, hence $\Delta_g=(4W^2)^{-1}\Delta$\\
Transported eigenproblem & $\Delta_{\Sph}Y=-l(l+1)Y\iff\Delta u=-l(l+1)(4W^2)u$\\
Chart identities & $\Delta\sigma_i=-2(4W^2)\sigma_i$, $\grad\sigma_i\cdot\grad\sigma_j=4W^2(\delta_{ij}-\sigma_i\sigma_j)$\\
Explicit patterns & $3+5+7$ verified independent eigenfunctions of degrees $1,2,3$\\
Multiplicity & $\binom{l+2}{2}-\binom{l}{2}=2l+1$\\
Spectral gain & $g(s)=se^{1-s}$ strictly unimodal, unique peak $g(1)=1$\\
Argmax bracketing & maximiser $\in\{\lfloor1/r\rfloor,\lceil1/r\rceil\}$ for all $r>0$\\
Strict selection & at $r=1/k$, degree $k$ uniquely maximises, $\lambda_k=1$\\
Floor formula fails & at $r=0.4$ the winner is $3=\lceil1/r\rceil$\\
Exact count & dimension exactly $2l+1$ for $l=1,2,3$ in the polynomial ansatz\\
North-pole obstruction & decay $\iff$ zonal coefficient $=0$; decaying dimension $2N$\\
Ray estimate & $|u-c|\le(2|a|+2|b|+2|c|)/R$ for $R\ge1$\\
Sectoral decay & $|\sigma_1^2-\sigma_2^2|\le8R^{-2}$, $|\sigma_1(\sigma_1^2-3\sigma_2^2)|\le32R^{-3}$\\
Rotational action & plane rotation $\leftrightarrow$ polar rotation; $N$-fold sectoral symmetry\\
Kelvin duality & inversion in the unit circle $\leftrightarrow$ equatorial reflection\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\end{document}

